0531"], ["Bash", "ls -la && head -5 contacts.csv && wc -l contacts.csv"], ["Bash", "cat -A contacts.csv; echo; awk -F, 'NR>1{e=tolower($2); gsub(/^[ \\t]+|[ \\t]+$/,\"\",e); g[e]=g[e] \"\\n  \" $0; c[e]++} END{for(k in c) if(c[k]>1) print k \" (\" c[k] \"x):\" g[k]}' contacts.csv"]], "final_message": "Look at the email column in `contacts.csv`. The file is small (3 contacts), and the problem shows up there.\n\nTwo rows are the same person with different casing:\n\n```\nAna Ruiz,ana@example.org\nana ruiz,ANA@example.org\n```\n\nAn exact-match dedupe treats `ana@example.org` and `ANA@example.org` as different addresses. Mail systems deliver both to the same inbox, so Ana gets the newsletter twice. Bo Lin appears once.\n\n**What to check**\n1. **Fix the data.** Lowercase and trim the email column, then dedupe on that. Here that means dropping one of the Ana rows.\n2. **Fix it at the source.** Find whatever imports or signs up contacts, such as a signup form or an import script. Make it normalize emails before saving, or enforce a unique constraint on the lowercased email. Otherwise the duplicates will come back.\n3. **Check other causes.** I only looked at the CSV, so I can't rule these out:\n   - Look at `crontab.txt` for a newsletter job that is scheduled twice or overlaps.\n   - Check whether the send ran twice or was retried after a partial failure.\n   - Check whether the list is merged with another list at send time.\n\nI haven't changed any files. I can write the cleaned CSV, or look at `crontab.txt` and the logs for a double send, if you want either.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}